101 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (We wanted our tool to be applicable for cities across the country. However, given the range of climates, one scale for temperatures in our reclassification scheme would loose meaning very quickly. If we compared Phoenix and Boston with the same scale, all we would be able to learn is that Boston is cooler than Phoenix. Python scripting helped us tackle this issue. For every city, we would find the average temperature and the standard deviation. We could then create a unique reclassification scheme using deviation from the mean, where cells would be compared to the average of the whole city. While this method makes it difficult to compare results of different cities, within one city, it makes areas of anomalously high temperatures clear to the user.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
102 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 74 0 R
/T (Outcome)
/E (Outcome)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
103 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (This tool is helpful for consolidating and summarizing multiple variables that contribute to urban heat islands. At a broad scale, it helps to see general trends across a city. Our example of two neighborhoods in Chicago, Hyde Park and Cicero, show that access to green space is uneven between neighborhoods.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
104 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (As heat is a leading environmental cause of illness, addressing the lack of green space, therefore mitigating urban heat islands, may be a strategy for landscape managers to investigate.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
