135 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (Our original goal was to investigate environmental impacts on neighborhood-level health outcomes. Looking back, cluster analysis would have helped us answer those questions. However, this class focused regression analysis, and clustering was not something we went over. We were somewhat limited by the tools available to us.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
136 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (Our data was also a major limitation. We have no way of knowing whether the power plants are the main source of air pollution. Even if it was, asthma and COPD are not perfect measures of the impact of that pollution. Individual variables, like genetic predisposition or smoking, would almost certainly be better predictors of respiratory health. The data available to us was too general to tell us much, but it was enough to open up some interesting questions.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
137 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 78 0 R
/T (Outcome)
/E (Outcome)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
138 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (Our project was an interesting exploration into spatial distributions of health. We did not find any evidence that individuals living closer to coal-burning power plants were any more likely to have asthma or COPD. Instead, areas with higher populations of Black individuals or lower median household incomes are more correlated with higher rates of respiratory illness. To better understand this correlation, we could investigate how areas of higher asthma and COPD prevalence are spatially arranged.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
139 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
